#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199294} :activity/id #uuid "2c7e700e-97c2-5568-92c3-9812910bbf91" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049184 :topic/id #uuid "e1a5b1bc-c302-556f-adf4-38f31ef47d34" :topic/math-academy-id 4144} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199468 :tutorial/content "Remember that when we measure the flux of a vector field through a *closed* surface, we always calculate with respect to an outward-pointing unit normal vector.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\,0, \\, -y^2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the conical solid shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0be83ceada68d0b56be4872f21652a7d18932ff059ea704576ca3cce892fa87b.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$ \n\nFirst, since $S$ is a closed surface, we have that\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 2 - \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$ is oriented *upward*, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = 0$ is oriented *downward*.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- First, we consider the upper cone $S_1.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = 0, \\qquad\nR = -y^2.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+(-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Next, we consider the lower plane $S_2.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,-1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨x,0,-{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the entire surface is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "73467c61-810e-5e81-abcc-b5eb99d1e0ab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7651 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1bb12849-8513-5a29-9551-63f2f3815039" :step/math-academy-id 44432 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198401 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198043 :question/id #uuid "fe87a57b-09fc-5c98-9d4c-e3ba3b95c2a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12419"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049315}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434400}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "766d1837-ad6a-5940-9b62-d25b3ab7823b" :step/math-academy-id 44618 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198402 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198044 :question/id #uuid "5fb5ffe1-51cb-5db8-b0a4-effd27cc623f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12420"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050005}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405118}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"} :step/id #uuid "df7f59a9-5505-5955-a72e-ed3764163143" :step/math-academy-id 44619}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198486}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481704} :activity/id #uuid "8d08405b-e533-59a3-974d-02400c4f8447" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049184 :topic/id #uuid "e1a5b1bc-c302-556f-adf4-38f31ef47d34" :topic/math-academy-id 4144} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46218ce4-ea58-5de1-86c0-d1c69e873ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279220} {:answer/id #uuid "4f46e641-3ee3-5376-98dd-a02b81e26818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279312} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b5b14-4c9b-524f-9436-0ac569357f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy-y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279717} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d59bc9-b571-564a-bff6-8c35092d9426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280086} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67d8e2-c014-5128-9067-69e0d0ab8a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280386} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ddaedb2-b68c-5509-aca4-917bce34c4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4e1f048-4c99-5435-999b-508c95c11d59" :question/math-academy-id "q-161899" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a48be0b4c101babf157a05959e923576ad5614113b3eec9e93fe747d5dc3803.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=x,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,x,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4fb77fcc-508c-53c1-9504-71147177444b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69acc573-b0b8-5874-aab1-a28efcd81a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19x-2{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371983} {:answer/id #uuid "59cdcb69-6653-5e15-899a-5aa388d46281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16x-x{y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371984} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfc894d-49bf-586f-9c15-0545c41d8169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371985} {:answer/id #uuid "79846a78-79c8-5a37-ae61-84fe6a556fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20x+2y{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371986} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c3b3f1-d211-5651-a1bd-4e92f6df7b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20x+{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371985} :answer-field/id #uuid "106248c3-5a77-5d72-aed7-25c25eb96f2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "71368570-4ef4-5d96-b173-947fce0b9458" :question/math-academy-id "q-161898" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2x)-(0)\\cdot (-2y)+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(18x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}19x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}19x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}18x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "619efe09-3031-5ed9-849a-5c2f5bf2ac5a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481704 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c3047a2-d5df-5a0c-ab0c-a0f194bdad31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278851} {:answer/id #uuid "332e5ac0-126d-59e7-a343-89d3450f7bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279037} {:answer/id #uuid "4371b2b7-1b47-5452-b1ab-806e3857aeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279196} {:answer/id #uuid "ab87156c-d08c-577f-aff1-abc00dced344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280110} {:answer/id #uuid "e2408eb1-f6f7-5772-bd58-8e40f81dd72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}-8y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279196} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a2ccf4-8749-57d6-a76d-0242f177c147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca709c99-e06d-5a3f-baf0-9520bf4a14bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161830" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "61d61754-7b4b-53fa-b355-e1e2744b21c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "855f9cab-5970-5823-b1ec-e6f8c8330244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405071} {:answer/id #uuid "feb823c9-ae29-56b1-bece-172402691eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405072} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9ee25f-ef8f-511d-86bf-e7005eafe934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405073} {:answer/id #uuid "6bef0962-a68e-52b4-9ae4-4e5699c26ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405074} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9eb018-8e9f-52c9-8219-0eed349fe16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405072} :answer-field/id #uuid "1886a1f4-707b-5268-9cc1-ed7effda4966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0d7ee053-a1f8-527b-9332-a3761ff9b930" :question/math-academy-id "q-161896" :question/problem "![](images/14/1422e6534c001214335dcddb8888f7e2e9344e440379c47da38514e9a3ef0bd8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,2⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q=0,\\,R=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7c21cb12-f528-579b-9e03-4e7e70d8e424" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c9ad26-77bc-5b7f-828d-2c0cdf073e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278645} {:answer/id #uuid "1152644c-483e-593c-b840-d414f08e1c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278753} {:answer/id #uuid "4268c067-2778-56fa-ab8e-30b1e87b9f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279189} {:answer/id #uuid "59529013-f848-5ed5-a847-a57d0ae0ecf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-5x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279411} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6a9216-3456-56b0-978c-fded9c69ed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280814} :answer-field/id #uuid "8306574e-b0dd-58d3-90c0-2fcec5bd2691" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca856459-2199-5fb8-97c9-ba0974b5b2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-161905" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aa2781e1-2aff-5e27-88ae-47fd0dd86c13"}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481575}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481676} :activity/id #uuid "db9efddc-376b-56c9-8c33-6e5ed67ba2f4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049184 :topic/id #uuid "e1a5b1bc-c302-556f-adf4-38f31ef47d34" :topic/math-academy-id 4144} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d747dec-4db3-5966-9a2d-772570a4c2a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278717} {:answer/id #uuid "2b49f721-d34f-57b8-887a-a07af8acd027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-6x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278976} {:answer/id #uuid "3db364b4-51c0-5078-8edc-2aab3af9d03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279134} {:answer/id #uuid "6a21fb2b-62f4-563c-8f2f-7175d9195a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279552} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5aa06e-86ef-5b2a-8470-8113b5d11a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279682} :answer-field/id #uuid "99a942db-f0af-510b-bd6b-aeb36a731348" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fca4156b-f8d5-50fa-a7b9-3894a3eed862" :question/math-academy-id "q-161895" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a0b986a-3b71-5b0e-9053-e82fa56190a8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b802cb74-ee56-571a-ba2b-74472252f1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405078} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7781c-10a9-5713-ab76-29cfaffd54d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405079} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6025cd-45c8-596b-b893-c5fd2b0d6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405080} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f9eadd-2663-5edf-a933-30b97b13faa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405081} {:answer/id #uuid "11453532-2079-5fc7-a118-668744b23d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405080} :answer-field/id #uuid "51599c73-a498-5c6e-99ec-3daf9c417dd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1fddd3a8-32de-55ef-b058-e60e47b22c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-161897" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6af3d84594e2b2648dcbbae05a64f03ef5a38c76f5a1ed835fc4c6b6f50ef97a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-x,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-x,\\,R=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(-x)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-x,-0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1a630c08-382a-5c3a-8869-8aab867411d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481734}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d997b13e-de60-5563-841f-2fd5ee29d99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405113} {:answer/id #uuid "c47bf5a7-ddbb-55ea-94c5-903a6bd874ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405114} {:answer/id #uuid "a933c2f8-9508-5563-b677-d1cf1467dfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405115} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7cafee-a688-51a7-b3d1-4d1daf466854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405116} {:answer/id #uuid "156642d0-8eb1-5036-896e-6fea2101a8de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405116} :answer-field/id #uuid "9854d104-9a4b-53d3-8285-a6f5f2377a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "883193fd-f5be-5d1c-8ffa-32b09d1431cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161908" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2fff2eab1f5670dfcd3351dcbb9be1b3110e6ebdcd452cf4c5a273611d454c8b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ae60978-ce20-5ee4-aae6-9abe617609aa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a8ff49-f833-5f3c-850a-8d217758437e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2{y}^{3}-12xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279047} {:answer/id #uuid "63e9c76d-0951-5e6a-9281-3e125cd1d310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2x+6y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279500} {:answer/id #uuid "9aac3735-e148-5a13-a799-508ff10d7ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279963} {:answer/id #uuid "b48c9db8-b76f-57f1-9490-1c2b1d9bda32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x{y}^{2}+2{y}^{3}+2x-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280183} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f78e2b-f03d-51a5-a61b-fc3d2913a4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2xy-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279963} :answer-field/id #uuid "61b875c5-3db3-5698-aae1-ec503104d82f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2fe585d2-13a2-5e4d-ba70-80b8560f5587" :question/math-academy-id "q-161911" :question/problem "![](images/57/5709af87720dcb052d8ffc6cc600c5889e45849aee6bed69ec3646e43c3cefb1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,1,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3,\\,Q=1,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3)\\cdot 2x-1\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-6x+2y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8980b678-c09e-511b-8f7a-7cdc3940cc2c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481734 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b0db8ab-29d7-5fe3-9f20-8a503e358b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278839} {:answer/id #uuid "2b094c28-da56-5d15-a274-fa1ad40311ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278971} {:answer/id #uuid "706d0a96-7dfe-5cc7-b55c-f78d8b793955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279612} {:answer/id #uuid "a892c1e1-5080-5e0f-8d79-ae3ae1774d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280080} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1bdc04-3a79-54f8-8b7e-e69a71a66f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278839} :answer-field/id #uuid "b28d0bd3-3943-5d91-91b9-a9ce611eece5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f651353-551c-55cb-9916-e19d1d757989" :question/math-academy-id "q-161907" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1+x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1+x$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x)=1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-1\\cdot 2x-0\\cdot 2y+(-x))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2x-x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1-x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x-1\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cfabf4bc-f80d-55cf-ba91-6de33824c78e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481709}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481578}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512980} :activity/id #uuid "fbb16356-5d9b-531b-bc04-17b88f44ef79" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049184 :topic/id #uuid "e1a5b1bc-c302-556f-adf4-38f31ef47d34" :topic/math-academy-id 4144} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263b1feb-971b-5d7f-8e46-5b3bc0e94dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405085} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4e563f-2961-582e-92cd-4ac00f3541da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405086} {:answer/id #uuid "94cdc7b9-f47b-525c-b5b6-ab08d22fded3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405087} {:answer/id #uuid "34969a97-52a8-5790-8d57-5135e6098783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405088} {:answer/id #uuid "c701af60-222c-5761-9908-aa65b644b188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405085} :answer-field/id #uuid "55ff700f-c93e-5ab0-80b1-0ec031f0502f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3eea45a-e598-5192-b757-2e1b9a1153d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161901" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7f140ad2c5687db48171819e1efaa858701af881ebbee57ff835da569f30947.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP={z}^{2}=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨{z}^{2},0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "24a4d735-bf7c-573f-9ba5-80873e983f63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e0745c0-c1e3-54ed-89c3-84df7cb535ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405099} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc9ee65-567a-55e7-8e17-a3faf479965a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405100} {:answer/id #uuid "1d66daf3-c66b-5610-a4a1-945ece3d502b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405101} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd338b1-bf95-5ed9-80a5-7996beec69f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405102} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2bc3ec-eeb8-52c4-94c5-3b19fba9b500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405099} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf9dad5-46da-52ad-9f58-b7b22be13881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "150a753b-c416-5943-8cfa-2d89a5b17df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161904" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-(0)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2ec90c17-0169-5fa1-a78b-19f41aa17a65"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69acc573-b0b8-5874-aab1-a28efcd81a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19x-2{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371983} {:answer/id #uuid "59cdcb69-6653-5e15-899a-5aa388d46281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16x-x{y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371984} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfc894d-49bf-586f-9c15-0545c41d8169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371985} {:answer/id #uuid "79846a78-79c8-5a37-ae61-84fe6a556fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20x+2y{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371986} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c3b3f1-d211-5651-a1bd-4e92f6df7b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20x+{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371985} :answer-field/id #uuid "106248c3-5a77-5d72-aed7-25c25eb96f2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "71368570-4ef4-5d96-b173-947fce0b9458" :question/math-academy-id "q-161898" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2x)-(0)\\cdot (-2y)+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(18x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}19x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}19x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}18x-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a200200-f9a3-5d67-b477-751312a3c4c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "855f9cab-5970-5823-b1ec-e6f8c8330244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405071} {:answer/id #uuid "feb823c9-ae29-56b1-bece-172402691eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405072} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9ee25f-ef8f-511d-86bf-e7005eafe934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405073} {:answer/id #uuid "6bef0962-a68e-52b4-9ae4-4e5699c26ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405074} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9eb018-8e9f-52c9-8219-0eed349fe16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405072} :answer-field/id #uuid "1886a1f4-707b-5268-9cc1-ed7effda4966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0d7ee053-a1f8-527b-9332-a3761ff9b930" :question/math-academy-id "q-161896" :question/problem "![](images/14/1422e6534c001214335dcddb8888f7e2e9344e440379c47da38514e9a3ef0bd8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,2⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z=1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,Q=0,\\,R=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-(1-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x+2\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "406ef78e-6b81-5dfd-92d8-7cecdbdc2a9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86b9be73-bab1-55d5-99f8-7629e8fdfac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405092} {:answer/id #uuid "03b65f71-c8ab-568a-a5b4-8a9e1e082302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405093} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ec3f6d-843d-5ada-bec6-0a99ea632881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405094} {:answer/id #uuid "62a9e402-0459-5909-abf2-55ce70fd1f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405095} {:answer/id #uuid "c486fa33-cd09-56ea-8bcb-3f7d7b5b1b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405094} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8800b1d-0dbb-5dd9-8914-c007ddb67ebc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c8fd3ad8-a86b-542f-ab4a-6226468a4f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161903" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-z,\\,2y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-z,2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d897879d-381d-5c1c-a360-cf41d1b97535" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512973}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512096}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541160} :activity/id #uuid "9d467660-a98d-5edb-bc42-02dab1e4b3ea" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049184 :topic/id #uuid "e1a5b1bc-c302-556f-adf4-38f31ef47d34" :topic/math-academy-id 4144} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c3047a2-d5df-5a0c-ab0c-a0f194bdad31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278851} {:answer/id #uuid "332e5ac0-126d-59e7-a343-89d3450f7bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279037} {:answer/id #uuid "4371b2b7-1b47-5452-b1ab-806e3857aeef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279196} {:answer/id #uuid "ab87156c-d08c-577f-aff1-abc00dced344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280110} {:answer/id #uuid "e2408eb1-f6f7-5772-bd58-8e40f81dd72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}y+2{y}^{3}-8y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280591}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279196} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a2ccf4-8749-57d6-a76d-0242f177c147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca709c99-e06d-5a3f-baf0-9520bf4a14bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161830" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0fb559ef-7e8d-533b-b198-27c986ee0421" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541194}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541178 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b802cb74-ee56-571a-ba2b-74472252f1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405078} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7781c-10a9-5713-ab76-29cfaffd54d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-xy\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405079} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6025cd-45c8-596b-b893-c5fd2b0d6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405080} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f9eadd-2663-5edf-a933-30b97b13faa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405081} {:answer/id #uuid "11453532-2079-5fc7-a118-668744b23d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405080} :answer-field/id #uuid "51599c73-a498-5c6e-99ec-3daf9c417dd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1fddd3a8-32de-55ef-b058-e60e47b22c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-161897" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6af3d84594e2b2648dcbbae05a64f03ef5a38c76f5a1ed835fc4c6b6f50ef97a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-x,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich expression belongs in the blank when the upper and lower surfaces are parametrized as graphs over $D$ and their outward flux integrands are added pointwise where defined, before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-x,\\,R=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(-x)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-x,-0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4db3fcce-be9d-5f42-a907-5f887c87d7cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541184 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c9ad26-77bc-5b7f-828d-2c0cdf073e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278645} {:answer/id #uuid "1152644c-483e-593c-b840-d414f08e1c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278753} {:answer/id #uuid "4268c067-2778-56fa-ab8e-30b1e87b9f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279189} {:answer/id #uuid "59529013-f848-5ed5-a847-a57d0ae0ecf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-5x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279411} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6a9216-3456-56b0-978c-fded9c69ed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280814} :answer-field/id #uuid "8306574e-b0dd-58d3-90c0-2fcec5bd2691" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca856459-2199-5fb8-97c9-ba0974b5b2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-161905" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨z,0,y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+10x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "59d7191b-c0b7-517e-bf2f-a21f70175ce1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541194 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d747dec-4db3-5966-9a2d-772570a4c2a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{x}^{3}-2x{y}^{2}+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278717} {:answer/id #uuid "2b49f721-d34f-57b8-887a-a07af8acd027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{x}^{3}-6x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278976} {:answer/id #uuid "3db364b4-51c0-5078-8edc-2aab3af9d03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279134} {:answer/id #uuid "6a21fb2b-62f4-563c-8f2f-7175d9195a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279552} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5aa06e-86ef-5b2a-8470-8113b5d11a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279682} :answer-field/id #uuid "99a942db-f0af-510b-bd6b-aeb36a731348" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fca4156b-f8d5-50fa-a7b9-3894a3eed862" :question/math-academy-id "q-161895" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e374f18d8b87de2fddd9a2127ee84709746c7c1b75083441afcc7b6c79402fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨-z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x+x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-z,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-9x)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}-10x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7d9afcbf-aa91-5aa1-9258-47bfadefbc7d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541178}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46218ce4-ea58-5de1-86c0-d1c69e873ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279220} {:answer/id #uuid "4f46e641-3ee3-5376-98dd-a02b81e26818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279312} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9b5b14-4c9b-524f-9436-0ac569357f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{xy-y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279717} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d59bc9-b571-564a-bff6-8c35092d9426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280086} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67d8e2-c014-5128-9067-69e0d0ab8a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280386} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ddaedb2-b68c-5509-aca4-917bce34c4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a4e1f048-4c99-5435-999b-508c95c11d59" :question/math-academy-id "q-161899" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a48be0b4c101babf157a05959e923576ad5614113b3eec9e93fe747d5dc3803.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=x,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,x,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "91e8a20b-e6de-543d-ae33-ca160809893a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541184}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540522}]}
